<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-assignment-9" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Assignment 9</title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the gradient to compute directional derivatives and
find directions of fastest change. They then find tangent planes to a
graph and to a surface, recover a gradient from a tangent plane, and use
a linearization to approximate a value. If you would like to review the
material first, see
<xref ref="sec-skel-fmv-directional-derivatives" text="title"/> and
Section 4.6, Tangent Planes and Differentials.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Directional Derivatives
     and Gradient Vectors": its odd-numbered problems ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-1">
<statement>
<p>
Consider <m>f(x,y) = e^{-r^4}</m> where <m>r = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
Compute its directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> with respect to the
unit vectors in (polar) directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>. What about any
other angle?
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The function is symmetric with respect to rotations around the origin,
so all the directional derivatives will be equal. Therefore it is
sufficient to compute the derivative for <m>\theta = 0</m>, i.e.
<m>\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial x}</m>:
<md>
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}
= \frac{\partial}{\partial x} e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}
= -2(x^2+y^2)\cdot 2x\, e^{-(x^2+y^2)^2}.
</md>
Plugging in <m>x = y = 0</m> we see that this is zero. Hence the
directional derivative at <m>(0,0)</m> is <m>0</m> in the directions
<m>\theta = 0, \dfrac{\pi}{4}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, and by the same
symmetry it is <m>0</m> for every other angle as well.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rev-gr-3">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> is a unit vector and <m>f</m>
has continuous second partials, then
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f(x,y) = f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2,
</md>
where <m>D^2_{\hat{u}} f = D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]</m>.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<answer>
<p>True.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
We have that
<md>
D^2_{\hat{u}} f
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[D_{\hat{u}} f(x,y)\right]
= D_{\hat{u}}\left[a f_x + b f_y\right].
</md>
This simplifies to
<md>
\langle a f_{xx} + b f_{xy},\; a f_{xy} + b f_{yy}\rangle
\cdot \langle a, b\rangle
= f_{xx}a^2 + 2f_{xy}ab + f_{yy}b^2.
</md>
Note we needed <m>f</m> to have continuous second partials to invoke
Clairaut's Theorem.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rm2-gr-2">
<title>A mountain lion on a mountain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A mountain lion runs on a mountain whose height above the point
<m>(x,y)</m> is <m>z = x^2 + \sin^2(xy)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at the fastest
possible rate? What is this fastest rate?
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
In which direction(s) should the mountain lion run from point
<m>(1, 0, 1)</m> so that the height is increasing at <em>half</em> of
the fastest possible rate?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The fastest possible rate of increase is attained in the direction of
the gradient:
<md>
\nabla z = \left\langle \frac{\partial z}{\partial x},
\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right\rangle
= \left\langle 2x + 2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,y,\;
2\sin(xy)\cos(xy)\,x\right\rangle.
</md>
At <m>(1, 0, 1)</m>, this gradient is simply
<m>\nabla z(1,0) = \langle 2, 0\rangle</m>, and the fastest possible
rate of change is the length of the gradient, i.e.
<m>\|\nabla z(1,0)\| = \|\langle 2,0\rangle\| = 2</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Half of the fastest possible rate of height increase at point
<m>(1,0,1)</m> is <m>\frac12 \cdot 2 = 1</m>. Let
<m>\hat{u} = \langle a, b\rangle</m> be a unit vector in the desired
direction. Then the directional derivative
<m>D_{\hat{u}}(1,0) = 1</m> is the desired rate of change of height at
<m>(1,0,1)</m> in the direction of <m>\hat{u}</m>; it can be calculated
using the dot product with the gradient:
<md>
1 = D_{\hat{u}}(1,0)
= \hat{u} \cdot \nabla z(1,0)
= \langle a, b\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0\rangle
= 2a + 0b = 2a.
</md>
Thus, <m>a = \frac12</m> and <m>b</m> must be such that
<m>\hat{u} = \left\langle \frac12, b\right\rangle</m> is unit, i.e.
<m>\left(\frac12\right)^2 + b^2 = 1</m>, from which
<m>b^2 = \frac34</m> and <m>b = \pm\frac{\sqrt3}{2}</m>. Therefore,
the desired directions in which the height is increasing at a rate
equal to half of the fastest possible rate are
<m>\hat{u}_1 = \left\langle \frac12, \frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>
and
<m>\hat{u}_2 = \left\langle \frac12, -\frac{\sqrt3}{2}\right\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-3">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y)</m> is a function of two variables and let
<md>
\hat u = \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1\rangle, \qquad
\hat v = \langle 0, 1\rangle.
</md>
Suppose that at a point <m>P_0(a,b)</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 0,</mrow>
<mrow>\left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} \amp = 1.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find <em>the direction</em> of the maximum rate of change at
<m>P_0(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Using
<m>\left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0}
= \nabla f\big|_{P_0}\cdot\hat u</m>, the two conditions become
<md>
<mrow>\amp \left(D_{\hat u} f\right)_{P_0} = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad \Rightarrow\; \langle f_x, f_y\rangle \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, -1\rangle = 0</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad \Rightarrow\; \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\left(f_x - f_y\right) = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; f_x = f_y,</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \left(D_{\hat v} f\right)_{P_0} = 1</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad \Rightarrow\; \langle f_x, f_y\rangle \cdot \langle 0, 1\rangle = f_y = 1.</mrow>
</md>
Hence <m>\nabla f\big|_{P_0} = \langle 1, 1\rangle</m>, and the
direction of the maximum rate of change at <m>P_0</m> is given by
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\nabla f\big|_{P_0}}{\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\|}
= \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\langle 1, 1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\left\|\nabla f\big|_{P_0}\right\| = \sqrt{1 + 1} = \sqrt2</m>
is the maximum rate of change.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw21-8">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^2 e^{3y}</m> and the point
<m>P_0 = (-1, 0)</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the direction in which <m>f(x,y)</m> decreases the most rapidly
at <m>P_0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the directional derivative of <m>f(x,y)</m> at <m>P_0</m> in the
direction given by <m>\overrightarrow{v} = \langle -1, 1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The gradient is
<md>
<mrow>\nabla f \amp = \left(2x e^{3y}\right)\hat{\imath} + \left(3x^2 e^{3y}\right)\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)} \amp = -2\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath},</mrow>
<mrow>\left\|\nabla f\big|_{(-1,0)}\right\| \amp = \sqrt{13}.</mrow>
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction of most rapid decrease is opposite to the gradient:
<md>
-\frac{\nabla f}{\|\nabla f\|}
= \left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\imath}
- \left(\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}\right)\hat{\jmath}
= \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{13}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}
\right\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The unit vector in the direction of <m>\overrightarrow{v}</m> is
<m>\hat u = \dfrac{\overrightarrow{v}}{\|\overrightarrow{v}\|}
= \left\langle -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle</m>,
so
<md>
D_{\hat u} f
= \nabla f \cdot \hat u
= \langle -2, 3\rangle \cdot
\left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}, \frac{1}{\sqrt2}\right\rangle
= \frac{5}{\sqrt2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-8">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose the temperature (in degrees Celsius) at a point
<m>(x,y)</m> is given by <m>T(x,y)</m>, where <m>T</m> is a
continuous function that has continuous partial derivatives, and
distances in the <m>xy</m>-plane are measured in meters. We are given
the following information about <m>T</m> at the point
<m>(x,y) = (1,1)</m>:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
<m>T(1,1) = 20</m> <degree/>C,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> obtains its smallest
(i.e. most negative) value in the direction toward the <em>point</em>
<m>(-1, 2)</m>,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> in the direction of the
<em>vector</em> <m>4\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath}</m> is
<m>3</m> <degree/>C/m.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the derivative of <m>T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m> in the direction
toward the <em>point</em> <m>(4,-3)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
If you start at <m>(1,1)</m> and move along a straight path toward
the point <m>(4,-3)</m>, <em>approximately</em> how far will you
have to go in this direction until the temperature reaches
<m>24</m> <degree/>C?
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
First we determine <m>\nabla T</m> at <m>(1,1)</m>. The derivative
is most negative in the direction of <m>-\nabla T</m>, and the
direction from <m>(1,1)</m> toward <m>(-1,2)</m> is
<md>
\langle -1-1,\; 2-1\rangle = \langle -2, 1\rangle.
</md>
Hence <m>-\nabla T</m> is a positive multiple of
<m>\langle -2, 1\rangle</m>, i.e.
<m>\nabla T = c\,\langle 2, -1\rangle</m> for some <m>c \gt 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
The third piece of information fixes <m>c</m>. The unit vector in the
direction of <m>4\,\hat{\imath} + 3\,\hat{\jmath}</m> is
<m>\frac15\langle 4, 3\rangle</m>, since
<m>\|\langle 4,3\rangle\| = 5</m>, so
<md>
3 = \nabla T \cdot \frac15\langle 4, 3\rangle
= \frac{c}{5}\Big(8 - 3\Big)
= c
\;\Rightarrow\; c = 3.
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\nabla T\big|_{(1,1)}
= 3\langle 2, -1\rangle
= \langle 6, -3\rangle.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The direction from <m>(1,1)</m> toward <m>(4,-3)</m> is
<m>\langle 3, -4\rangle</m>, whose length is <m>5</m>, so the unit
vector is <m>\hat u = \left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle</m>
and
<md>
\left(D_{\hat u} T\right)_{(1,1)}
= \langle 6, -3\rangle \cdot
\left\langle \frac35, -\frac45\right\rangle
= \frac{18}{5} + \frac{12}{5}
= 6 \; {}^{\circ}\text{C}/\text{m}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The temperature must rise from <m>20</m> <degree/>C to <m>24</m>
<degree/>C, i.e. <m>\Delta T = 4</m> <degree/>C, and along this
direction it rises at approximately <m>6</m> <degree/>C per meter.
Using the linear approximation
<m>\Delta T \approx \left(D_{\hat u}T\right) \cdot s</m>, where
<m>s</m> is the distance travelled,
<md>
s \approx \frac{\Delta T}{D_{\hat u}T} = \frac46 = \frac23
\approx 0.67 \text{ meters}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<!-- ===== From the old worksheet "Exercises for Tangent Planes and
     Differentials" ===== -->
<exercise xml:id="rev-tp-1">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation for the tangent plane to the graph of
<m>f(x,y) = \cos(xy)</m> passing through the point
<m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>f_x = -y\sin(xy)</m> and <m>f_y = -x\sin(xy)</m>, so the
tangent plane at a general point <m>(x_0, y_0)</m> is given by
<md>
z - \cos(x_0 y_0)
= -y_0 \sin(x_0 y_0)(x - x_0)
- x_0 \sin(x_0 y_0)(y - y_0).
</md>
</p>

<p>
Plugging in our point <m>\left(\dfrac{\pi}{2}, 1, 0\right)</m>, this
becomes
<md>
z = -1\cdot\left(x - \frac{\pi}{2}\right)
- \frac{\pi}{2}\cdot(y - 1),
</md>
or equivalently
<md>
x + \frac{\pi}{2}\,y + z = \pi.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-9">
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation of the tangent plane at the point <m>P_0(2,0,2)</m>
on the surface <m>2z - x^2 = 0</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>w(x,y,z) = 2z - x^2 = 0</m>. Then
<md>
\nabla w = (-2x)\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf k,
</md>
and the normal to the tangent plane is
<md>
\vec n = \nabla w\big|_{P_0} = -4\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf k.
</md>
The constant is
<md>
D = \vec n \cdot \vec{OP_0}
= \langle -4, 0, 2\rangle \cdot \langle 2, 0, 2\rangle
= -8 + 4 = -4,
</md>
so from <m>Ax + By + Cz = D</m>, the tangent plane at
<m>P_0(2,0,2)</m> is
<md>
-4x + 2z = -4
\qquad\text{or}\qquad
2x - z = 2.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rf-8-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
Any tangent plane to a graph must meet that graph at exactly one
point.
</p>
<p>
Justify your answer fully: give a proof if the statement is true, or a
counterexample if it is false.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<answer>
<p>False.</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
Consider the plane <m>z = c</m>. The tangent
plane to this graph at any point is the same as <m>z = c</m>, so it
meets the graph at infinitely many points.
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-34">
<statement>
<p>
The tangent plane to <m>z = f(x,y)</m> at the point <m>(1,2,9)</m> is
<m>z = 3x + 5y - 4</m>. Find <m>\nabla f(1,2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The tangent plane to the graph of <m>f</m> at <m>(a,b)</m> is
<md>
z = f(a,b) + f_x(a,b)(x-a) + f_y(a,b)(y-b),
</md>
so once it is expanded the coefficient of <m>x</m> is
<m>f_x(a,b)</m> and the coefficient of <m>y</m> is
<m>f_y(a,b)</m>. Comparing with <m>z = 3x + 5y - 4</m> gives
<md>
f_x(1,2) = 3, \qquad f_y(1,2) = 5,
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\nabla f(1,2) = 3\,\mathbf i + 5\,\mathbf j.
</md>
(As a check, the plane passes through <m>(1,2,9)</m>:
<m>3(1) + 5(2) - 4 = 9</m> <m>\checkmark</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-36">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>f(x,y) = 2 + \ln(1 + 3x - 4y)</m>. Use a linearization to
approximate <m>f(0.1, 0.2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<!-- SOLUTION-LOCKED assignment="9" due="2026-11-18"
<solution>
<p>
The point <m>(0.1, 0.2)</m> is close to the origin, where the
function is easy to work with, so we linearize at <m>(0,0)</m>:
<md>
f(0,0) = 2 + \ln(1) = 2,
\qquad
f_x = \frac{3}{1+3x-4y},
\qquad
f_y = \frac{-4}{1+3x-4y},
</md>
so <m>f_x(0,0) = 3</m> and <m>f_y(0,0) = -4</m>. The linearization is
<md>
L(x,y) = f(0,0) + f_x(0,0)x + f_y(0,0)y = 2 + 3x - 4y,
</md>
and therefore
<md>
f(0.1, 0.2) \approx L(0.1, 0.2) = 2 + 0.3 - 0.8 = 1.5.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
END SOLUTION-LOCKED -->
</exercise>

</worksheet>